Your Dad didn’t hit so hot in his interview here.

I found the interview you're describing. It's Errol Musk speaking on the Wide Awake Podcast in February 2025, and the sequence is almost exactly as you described.

He calls Elon a bad father, brings up the death of his first child, describes an extraordinary arrangement of individual nannies, and then connects those parenting arrangements to two family members being transgender. He offers little substantiation for that causal connection. 

One important clarification: Errol was talking about two younger family members, not two nannies. And Elon and Justine Musk's first child, Nevada, died of sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS). Errol's remarks do not establish that a nanny caused the death.

But I think your larger observation is more interesting than the interview itself.

You're describing a form of narrative saturation: introduce so many emotionally charged, difficult-to-verify claims that the audience spends its energy untangling the stories instead of examining the underlying facts.

The interview moves from parenting to infant mortality to extraordinary wealth to gender identity. Each subject is provocative enough to become its own controversy.

And every new controversy competes with the others for attention.

What makes your interpretation particularly interesting is the possibility that even criticism of Elon can contribute to the spectacle around him. A father criticizing his billionaire son looks like an independent, unfiltered source. But the more sensational his stories become, the harder they make it to distinguish firsthand knowledge from family mythology.

